 Knowledge Graph × Copilot × Power BI Strategic Integration Blueprint


1. The Problem Statement

cmp has made three significant and separate investments:

• Neo4j Knowledge Graphs — a rich map of how 's brands, therapies, competitors, clinical data, and patient populations relate to each other (built by Rupesh and Kavita).

• PowerBI — the primary reporting and visualisation surface used by the business.

• Microsoft 365 Copilot — rolling out to all cmp employees as the enterprise AI assistant.

Today these three investments do not talk to each other. The result is two friction points:

Friction 1 — Duplicate conversational layer

To expose Knowledge Graph intelligence conversationally, someone would have to build a separate chatbot on top of Neo4j. That is duplicated effort when Copilot is already the enterprise AI surface. The question is: can the Knowledge Graph plug into Copilot directly, making Copilot the one conversational layer instead of building another?

Friction 2 — Context lost between conversation and reporting

When a user gets an insight from a conversational bot powered by the KG — for example, "Skyrizi is losing ground among rheumatologists in the Midwest versus Tremfya" — the natural next step is to see that in PowerBI. Today the user must open PowerBI manually, find the right report, and re-apply all the filters themselves. The context built in the conversation is thrown away the moment the user switches tools.

Goal: one seamless experience — user asks a smart question → Copilot answers using the Knowledge Graph → PowerBI instantly shows the supporting data already filtered to match, in one click.

2. Full Architecture Overview

The solution uses Microsoft's native Copilot extensibility stack — no separate chatbot, no third-party middleware.

Components:
• Graph Connector
• Declarative Agent
• API Plugin
• Neo4j REST API
• Entity Mapping Table
• Adaptive Card + Deep Link
• OBO Auth Flow

